{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks a straightforward arithmetic question predicated on the given rate. SRC_001 refuses to commit to the answer as a logical entailment, instead framing $6 as a mere empirical projection and hedging it with counterconditions about bulk discounts, loss-leaders, and transaction limits. For a rate problem of this kind, the standard mathematical convention is that the stated price applies uniformly unless otherwise specified; inventing speculative store policies to undermine the answer misrepresents the problem's scope and fails to provide the determinate solution it demands.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 has the arithmetic ($6) but explicitly refuses to treat it as deductively guaranteed, hedging the claim as merely defeasible by invoking hypothetical minimum-order fees, nonlinear bulk discounts, and one-bundle-per-customer limits. The problem is a standard unit-rate word problem that presupposes the advertised price generalizes to the requested quantity; introducing unannounced store policies to avoid a firm answer mischaracterizes the task and withholds the determinate conclusion the problem requires.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}